## THE OPEN BUG (resume here)

### Symptom
`oracle_spend_verify.rs` prints `COVENANT FAILED -- VerifyError`. The failing
line is the final require:
```
require(tx.outputs[0].scriptPubKey == byte[](newContractLock));
```

### Root cause (confirmed via cli-debugger)
The covenant rebuilds its next-state contract with:
```
newContract = 0x08 + byte[8](updated) + this.activeScriptPubKey.split(9).1
```
Under a **P2SH spend**, `this.activeScriptPubKey` resolves to the **P2SH locking
script** of the spent UTXO (`OP_BLAKE2B <32-byte hash> OP_EQUAL` = `aa20...87`,
35 bytes), NOT the redeem (contract) script. So `split(9).1` returns the back
end of the P2SH wrapper, not the contract body.

Debugger proof (v3, update(5)): `newContract` came out as 37 bytes:
```
[0x08,0x69,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00, <28 bytes of P2SH-hash tail ending 0x87>]
```
i.e. push-8 + 105 + seven zeros + (P2SH spk bytes 9..). That is the wrong base
to rebuild from, so its hash never equals the intended rep=105 contract hash.

### Why the copied example misled us
`simulating_state.sil` (the template we adapted) uses `activeScriptPubKey`
assuming it IS the contract code. That holds in a non-P2SH / bare-script context
but NOT for a P2SH covenant spend, which is how we deployed.

